null,"author_association":"NONE","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Dhondtguido/CalculiX/issues/57","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Consider a rectangular bar 100x100x3000 mm, made of steel (E=210000 MPa, v=0.28), fixed at one end and with a compressive force applied at the other end. When the load is smaller than the buckling load or close to it (1 N or Euler critical load of 470437.10 N applied), the results of a linear buckling analysis are correct. But when the applied load is significantly larger than the buckling load (e.g. 980665 N), the buckling factor might be predicted incorrectly (4.37 in this case which should be the second eigenvalue). The issue seems to be mesh- and solver-dependent. Spooles may even provide different results for each run when more than 2 processors are used.\r\n\r\nI shared the input file [here](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/ga6hyot7lfy3dxv40bo3v/Buckling_issue.inp?rlkey=95wrocz0vcsrx2b9aw0ws8hg2&dl=0).","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Dhondtguido/CalculiX/issues/74/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Dhondtguido/CalculiX/issues/74/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}